# agent-sync — you are probably not alone in this repository

This project may run several coding agents at once. If it contains
`.claude/agent-sync.json`, the rules below are not optional.

There are no enforcement hooks outside Claude Code. Nothing will stop you from
overwriting another agent's work — you have to run the checks yourself, and the run
is recorded `ungated` so nobody mistakes your session for a protected one.

Let SKILL be the installed skill directory (`~/.agents/skills/agent-sync`).

## Before you touch anything

    python3 "$SKILL/scripts/agent_sync.py" status

Read the two awareness blocks it prints: which other runs hold which tasks and in
which repository, and what changed since you last looked. Do not take on a task
another run holds.

## Before editing a shared register

Decision logs, roadmaps, boards, dependency tables and open-question registers are
written by everyone. Take a lease first, and check:

    python3 "$SKILL/scripts/agent_sync.py" acquire <TASK-ID>
    python3 "$SKILL/scripts/agent_sync.py" guard docs/DECISIONS.md

Exit 2 means **this run holds no lease** — not that somebody else holds that file. Do
not edit anyway. Release on every path, including failure — an abandoned lease looks
like active work until it expires.

## Ids are reserved, not read

Reading a "next free id" line is not reserving it; two agents read the same number.

    python3 "$SKILL/scripts/agent_sync.py" reserve DEC

## Two documentation sources

Git documents say how it *should* be. The as-built record says how it *actually* is.
Before starting a task, run `reconcile` and resolve every divergence. After finishing,
`record` what you built, update the git documents, and `reconcile` again.

    python3 "$SKILL/scripts/agent_sync.py" reconcile
    python3 "$SKILL/scripts/agent_sync.py" record "what you built" --decision DEC-0001

Full documentation: https://github.com/ssheleg/agent-sync
